Let the total distance be $3 x$. The time taken for each part is $ t_{1}=\frac{x}{10}, t_{2}=\frac{x}{20}$, and $t_{3}=\frac{x}{60}$. The average speed is the total distance divided by the total time.
$$
\begin{gathered}
v_{a v g}=\frac{\text { Total Distance }}{\text { Total Time }}=\frac{3 x}{t_{1}+t_{2}+t_{3}}=\frac{3 x}{\frac{x}{10}+\frac{x}{20}+\frac{x}{60}} \\
v_{a v g}=\frac{3}{\frac{6+3+1}{60}}=\frac{3 \times 60}{10}=18 \mathrm{~km} / \mathrm{h}
\end{gathered}
$$

The weight of the mass is $W=10 \mathrm{~kg} . \mathrm{wt}$. The junction point is in equilibrium. We resolve forces into horizontal and vertical components.
- Vertical Equilibrium ( $\Sigma F_{y}=0$ ): The upward vertical component of $T_{1}$ balances the downward weight.
$$
T_{1} \sin \left(60^{\circ}\right)-W=0 \Longrightarrow T_{1} \frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}=10 \Longrightarrow T_{1}=\frac{20}{\sqrt{3}} \mathrm{~kg} \cdot \mathrm{wt}
$$
- Horizontal Equilibrium ( $\Sigma F_{x}=0$ ): The rightward horizontal component of $T_{1}$ balances the leftward tension $T_{2}$.
$$
\begin{gathered}
T_{1} \cos \left(60^{\circ}\right)-T_{2}=0 \Longrightarrow T_{2}=T_{1} \cos \left(60^{\circ}\right) \\
T_{2}=\left(\frac{20}{\sqrt{3}}\right) \times \frac{1}{2}=\frac{10}{\sqrt{3}} \mathrm{~kg} \cdot \mathrm{wt}
\end{gathered}
$$

The work done against friction is $W_{f}=f_{k} \cdot d$. The frictional force is $f_{k}=\mu_{k} N$. On a horizontal surface, the normal force $N=m g$.
$$
\begin{gathered}
W_{f}=\left(\mu_{k} m g\right) d \\
W_{f}=(0.3)(50)(10)(20)=3000 \mathrm{~J}
\end{gathered}
$$

For a variable force, work done is the integral of the force over the displacement.
$$
\begin{gathered}
W=\int_{x_{1}}^{x_{2}} F(x) d x=\int_{0}^{2}\left(5-2 x+3 x^{2}\right) d x \\
W=\left[5 x-x^{2}+x^{3}\right]_{0}^{2} \\
W=\left(5(2)-2^{2}+2^{3}\right)-(0)=10-4+8=14 \mathrm{~J}
\end{gathered}
$$

At the lowest point, the net force towards the center provides the centripetal force: $T-m g=\frac{m v^{2}}{R}$.
$$
\begin{gathered}
T_{\text {low }}=\frac{m v^{2}}{R}+m g \\
T_{\text {low }}=\frac{1 \times 6^{2}}{1}+1 \times 10=36+10=46 \mathrm{~N}
\end{gathered}
$$

The equation for tension at an angle $\theta$ from the bottom is derived from the net centripetal force: $T-m g \cos \theta=\frac{m v^{2}}{L}$.
$$
\begin{gathered}
T=m g \cos \theta+\frac{m v^{2}}{L} \\
T=(2)(10) \cos 60^{\circ}+\frac{2 \times 5^{2}}{2.5} \\
T=20(0.5)+\frac{50}{2.5}=10+20=30 \mathrm{~N}
\end{gathered}
$$

