The electric field at the center of a semicircular arc with charge $Q$ and radius $R$ is given by $E= \frac{1}{4 \pi \epsilon_0} \frac{2 \lambda}{R}$, where $\lambda=Q /(\pi R)$.
1. Top half $(+q)$ : The field points downwards $(-\hat{j})$.
$$
\begin{gathered}
E_{\text {top }}=\frac{1}{4 \pi \epsilon_0} \frac{2(q / \pi R)}{R}=\frac{q}{2 \pi^2 \epsilon_0 R^2} \\
E_{\text {top }}=-\frac{q}{2 \pi^2 \epsilon_0 R^2} \hat{j}
\end{gathered}
$$
2. Bottom half $(-q)$ : The field also points downwards $(-\hat{j})$ because the field lines point towards negative charges.
$$
\begin{gathered}
E_{\text {bottom }}=\frac{1}{4 \pi \epsilon_0} \frac{2(q / \pi R)}{R}=\frac{q}{2 \pi^2 \epsilon_0 R^2} \\
E_{\text {bottom }}=-\frac{q}{2 \pi^2 \epsilon_0 R^2} \hat{j}
\end{gathered}
$$
3. Total field: The fields add up vectorially.
$$
E_{\text {total }}=E_{\text {top }}+E_{\text {bottom }}=-\frac{q}{2 \pi^2 \epsilon_0 R^2} \hat{j}-\frac{q}{2 \pi^2 \epsilon_0 R^2} \hat{j}=-\frac{q}{\pi^2 \epsilon_0 R^2} \hat{j}
$$

