Since there are no external torques, the angular momentum of the system is conserved. $L_{i}=L_{f} \Longrightarrow I_{i} \omega_{i}=I_{f} \omega_{f}$.
- Initial State: The beads are at the center ( $r=0$ ), so they have no moment of inertia. The initial moment of inertia is just that of the rod.
$$
I_{i}=I_{r o d}=\frac{1}{12} M L^{2}
$$
The initial angular velocity is $\omega_{i}=\omega_{0}$.
- Final State: The beads are at the ends of the rod, at a distance $r=L / 2$ from the center.
$$
\begin{gathered}
I_{f}=I_{\text {rod }}+I_{\text {beads }}=\frac{1}{12} M L^{2}+2 \times m\left(\frac{L}{2}\right)^{2} \\
I_{f}=\frac{1}{12} M L^{2}+2 m \frac{L^{2}}{4}=\frac{1}{12} M L^{2}+\frac{1}{2} m L^{2}=\left(\frac{M}{12}+\frac{6 m}{12}\right) L^{2}=\frac{M+6 m}{12} L^{2}
\end{gathered}
$$
Applying conservation of angular momentum:
$$
\begin{gathered}
\left(\frac{1}{12} M L^{2}\right) \omega_{0}=\left(\frac{M+6 m}{12} L^{2}\right) \omega_{f} \\
M \omega_{0}=(M+6 m) \omega_{f} \Longrightarrow \omega_{f}=\frac{M}{M+6 m} \omega_{0}
\end{gathered}
$$

